Give architecture for core java project,logic use in console framework etc.

The best approach is to build a framework-independent Core Java module and
reuse it through different application layers: console, desktop GUI, REST API, or other Java frameworks.

This architecture is often called Layered Architecture or,
with stronger separation of business rules from infrastructure, Clean Architecture.

## 1. Overall architecture

### Presentation / Application Entry Points

Console
Main.java

REST API
Spring Boot

Desktop GUI
Swing / JavaFX

Other clients
Future adapters

## Application / Service Layer
`BankService`, `AccountService`
Coordinates use cases and business operations

## Core Domain — Vanilla Java
`BankAccount`, `Money`, `BusinessRules`
Business logic, validation, calculations and domain models
No framework dependency

### Infrastructure / Data Layer
`AccountRepository`, JDBC, files, database
Storage and external integrations

The important principle is that your business logic should not depend on the console UI or Spring Boot.
Each entry point invokes the same core functionality.

## 2. Recommended project folder structure

For a banking application:

```
BankingSystem/
│
├── core/
│   └── src/main/java/com/app/core/
│       ├── domain/
│       │   ├── BankAccount.java
│       │   └── Transaction.java
│       ├── service/
│       │   └── BankService.java
│       ├── repository/
│       │   └── AccountRepository.java
│       └── validation/
│           └── AccountValidator.java
│
├── console-app/
│   └── src/main/java/com/app/console/
│       └── Main.java
│
├── spring-api/
│   └── src/main/java/com/app/api/
│       └── BankController.java
│
├── desktop-app/
│   └── src/main/java/com/app/desktop/
│       └── BankWindow.java
│
└── pom.xml
```

- core: reusable Java logic, with no dependency on UI frameworks.
- console-app: menu, input and output.
- spring-api: HTTP endpoints and request/response handling.
- desktop-app: graphical user interface.
- repository: an abstraction for storing and retrieving accounts.

You can start with just `core` and `console-app`. Add the other modules when you need them.

## 3. How a single operation flows

For example, when a user deposits ₹500:

Console or REST controller

Receives the amount: ₹500

BankService

Finds the account and coordinates the deposit

BankAccount

Validates the amount and updates the balance

Repository

Persists the updated account when storage is configured

Both console and REST API clients follow the same business rules. Only the way users interact with the application changes.

## 4. Dependency rules

| Component               | Can depend on                                |
| ----------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| Console UI              | Core services                                |
| Spring Boot controller  | Core services                                |
| Desktop UI              | Core services                                |
| Service layer           | Domain classes and repository interfaces     |
| Domain layer            | Plain Java types and other domain classes    |
| Database implementation | Repository interfaces and database libraries |

Avoid: putting `System.out.println()`, `Scanner`, `@RestController`, or Spring-specific annotations inside your domain classes. Keep input/output and framework code at the edges.

## 5. How to build it progressively

1. Phase 1 — Vanilla Java: Build the domain classes and service layer; run them from a console application.
2. Phase 2 — Persistence: Introduce a repository interface and implement it using file storage or JDBC.
3. Phase 3 — REST API: Create a Spring Boot module that depends on the core module.
4. Phase 4 — Testing: Test business logic independently using JUnit or plain Java tests.
5. Phase 5 — Reuse: Package the core module as a JAR and reuse it in other compatible Java applications.

One important detail: console, desktop, and REST interfaces can reuse the same core logic,
but they don't necessarily share the same running application or memory.
If they run as separate processes, shared account data needs persistent storage or a server.

My recommendation:
use a multi-module Maven project with a plain Java `core` module and a `console-app` module first.
This keeps the architecture simple while making it straightforward to add Spring Boot later.